\begin{hint}{ex:composite}
The arrows after $\ker(gf)$ are induced by $f$, by the quotient $B\to B/f(A)$, by $g$, and by the quotient $C/gf(A)\to C/g(B)$. Check that the map induced by $g$ is well defined. The snake lemma also applies to the rows $A\to B\to\operatorname{coker}f\to0$ and $0\to C\xrightarrow{\mathrm{id}}C\to0$; its connecting arrow starts at $\operatorname{coker}f$.
\end{hint}

\begin{hint}{ex:qnotproj}
The integer image of a rational number would be divisible by every positive integer. For the decomposition, split a denominator into relatively prime prime powers and apply Bézout's identity.
\end{hint}

\begin{hint}{ex:tensoralgebra}
First tensor the presentation of $R[X]/(p)$ and use right exactness. Over $\C$, factor $X^2+1$ and apply the Chinese remainder theorem. Over $\Q(\sqrt2)$, test whether $(a+b\sqrt2)^2$ can equal $3$ for rational $a,b$.
\end{hint}

\begin{hint}{ex:fivelemma}
For injectivity, send $x\in\ker h_3$ to $A_4$, then lift it back through $A_2$. For surjectivity, send $y\in B_3$ to $B_4$, lift through $h_4$, and correct the remaining error through $B_2$.
\end{hint}

\begin{hint}{ex:v10new-ch03-quotients}
The map should send $\bar a\otimes\bar b$ to $\overline{ab}$. The proposed inverse sends $\bar r$ to $\bar1\otimes\bar r$. Check separately what happens to a summand from $I$ and one from $J$.
\end{hint}

\begin{hint}{ex:v10new-ch03-snake}
If $\bar k$ lies in the kernel, $m$ divides $nk$. Lift it by the integer $k$, multiply by $n$, and pull back through multiplication by $m$. The final answer is a class modulo $n$.
\end{hint}

\begin{hint}{ex:v10new-ch03-idempotent}
Write $r=er+(1-e)r$ and multiply an element of the intersection by $e$. The tensor map is $er\otimes m\mapsto erm$; its inverse on $eM$ sends $em$ to $e\otimes em$.
\end{hint}

\begin{hint}{ex:v10new-ch03-finiterank}
A tensor uses only finitely many vectors of $W$. Choose a basis of their span and rewrite the tensor using that basis. Independent output vectors force the corresponding input functionals to vanish separately.
\end{hint}

solution_blocks:
\begin{solution}{ex:whichsplit}
\idea Use a section when a lift of the quotient generator has the correct order. Otherwise compare the middle term with the direct sum a splitting would require.
\writeup In the first sequence $s(\bar1)=\bar4\in\Z/6$ defines a section: $3\bar4=0$ and $\bar4$ maps to $\bar1$ modulo $3$. Thus it splits.

The second would require $\Z/9\cong\Z/3\oplus\Z/3$. The right side is killed by $3$, but the class of $1$ on the left is not. Thus it does not split. The third would require $\Z^2\cong\Z^2\oplus\Z/5$, which is impossible because the right side has non-zero torsion and the left side is torsion-free.

For the last sequence, choose $b\in\Z^2$ with $v(b)=1$. The map $s(k)=kb$ is a section, so it splits. The kernel is a free rank-one group: if $v(x,y)=ax+by$, surjectivity says $(a,b)=1$, and $K$ is generated by $(-b,a)$. This also checks directly that the middle group has the required direct-sum form.
\end{solution}

